# Fictional EP Tracklist

## 1. Eight Strings Out, Seven Back

**Story & Context:** The debut runs at 168 BPM but spends most of its first two minutes in a halftime 84 BPM feel. Thiri opens with an eight-note OUTBOUND saung phrase moving from low to high register. She reaches the highest structural note, pauses for less than a beat, then returns through seven notes rather than eight, leaving the phrase slightly asymmetrical even though its direction is mirrored.

Owen writes a four-bar drum figure that accumulates ghost hits toward the TURN. The first bar contains only kick and snare; the second adds one rim; the third adds two filtered break fragments; the fourth becomes the densest. After the TURN, the next four bars subtract those details in reverse order.

Mya places one prepared-electric-piano fifth exactly at the TURN and nowhere else in the opening section. Htet Lin adds one dry tuned-drum tone every second OUTBOUND pass. Ruth enters on the INBOUND half with a low contrabass-clarinet fourth, giving the return more mass than the departure.

At the midpoint, Thiri changes the return to six notes while keeping the outbound path at eight. The formal mirror remains visible, but its lengths no longer match. The track ends with one high string, one low string, and no final drum fill.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Overhead view of a curved Burmese harp beside a cream shipping manifest, eight small red marks climbing one side of a drawn arc and seven descending the other, black lacquer tray and brass scale in shallow focus. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “eight out,” “seven back,” “turn here,” and “same curve, shorter return.”

## 2. Manifest 42 Folds at the Snare

**Story & Context:** At 172 BPM, Owen uses the EP’s most detailed breakbeat programming, but the main snare still implies halftime. Thiri plays a six-note low-to-high path followed by a six-note return with one inner pitch changed, so the shape is symmetrical while the harmony is not.

Mya uses clipped electric-piano suspended seconds every four bars. Htet Lin plays a three-drum rising figure only during the OUTBOUND half and reverses drum order on the INBOUND half. Ruth contributes short contrabass-clarinet air bursts rather than notes for the first minute, then introduces one low pedal at the central snare hit.

The main structural event is an eight-bar section where Owen mirrors only density, not exact hits. The outbound bars become progressively busier; after the snare TURN the inbound bars become progressively emptier, but none of the individual drum events repeat in reverse. The listener hears formal symmetry without obvious copy-and-paste reversal.

The final minute removes the sub entirely. Harp, dry snare, tuned drums, and prepared piano continue with the same directional form, proving that the Curve Mirror does not depend on low-frequency weight.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Cream cargo manifest stamped 42 folded sharply through a printed snare marker, curved harp-string diagram on one side and mirrored percussion-density blocks on the other. Spoken fragments limited to “forty-two,” “fold at the snare,” “not the same notes,” and “same direction back.”

## 3. River Door Opens on the Inbound Half

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 162 BPM and begins with the sparsest break on the EP. Thiri plays widely spaced low saung notes, moving upward only one string group every two bars. Owen uses one kick, one dry snare, and faint ghost-noise tails. The full OUTBOUND journey takes nearly a minute.

At the TURN, an actual warehouse loading door is opened during the room recording, allowing river air and distant mechanical noise into the restrained room microphones. No sample is triggered; the acoustic environment simply becomes wider for the INBOUND half.

Ruth enters with one long contrabass-clarinet fundamental. Mya plays no chords, only isolated damped octaves. Htet Lin taps one high tuned drum after every second returning harp note. Owen gradually increases sub weight while reducing upper break detail, so the return becomes heavier but quieter in the high frequencies.

During the final passage, Thiri reaches the lowest string group and does not reverse again. The river-door ambience continues for several seconds after the last dry snare, then the recording cuts without fade.

